# Setting Up the Virtual Environment

This guide explains how to set up a local IAM environment using Oracle VirtualBox (or another virtualization platform if you prefer).

For this setup, I am using a desktop computer and a laptop:
- Desktop: 64 GB RAM, 16-core CPU
- Laptop: 16 GB RAM, 14-core CPU

The desktop will host the domain controller and database server, while the laptop will act as the client machine.

## Hardware Requirements

Before proceeding, make sure your host machine meets the following minimum requirements:

- At least 32 GB RAM
- At least 10 CPU cores
- Enough free storage to support multiple Windows VMs

If you have a powerful machine, you may be able to host everything on a single system. However, splitting the environment across multiple machines is recommended for better performance and easier management.

## Supported Operating Systems

This environment is built using Windows only. At this time, support is limited to Windows-based systems.

## Tools Used

For this setup, I will use:
- Microsoft VS Code for Windows
- Oracle VirtualBox
- Windows Server 2022 ISO

You can download VS Code here:
https://code.visualstudio.com/download

## Step 1: Install Oracle VirtualBox

Download the latest version of Oracle VirtualBox for Windows hosts from the official download page:

https://www.virtualbox.org/wiki/Downloads

Install VirtualBox on the machine you plan to use for your IAM environment.

## Step 2: Verify the Installation

After installation, verify that VirtualBox was installed correctly.

Open VS Code or another terminal/PowerShell window and check the VirtualBox executable path:

- Default path: `C:\Program Files\Oracle\VirtualBox\VBoxManage.exe`

If it is not there, open File Explorer and navigate to:
- `C:\Program Files`
- Then open the `Oracle` folder
- Look for the `VirtualBox` directory

If VirtualBox was installed normally, the executable should be in this location.

You can also verify the installation by running:

```powershell
& "C:\Program Files\Oracle\VirtualBox\VBoxManage.exe" --version
```

## Step 3: Verify the Virtual Ethernet Adapter and Identify the IP Address

Run the command below to view all network adapters. If VirtualBox was installed correctly, there should be a host-only Ethernet adapter present.

```powershell
ipconfig /all
```

You can also check the host-only adapters directly with this command:

```powershell
"C:\Program Files\Oracle\VirtualBox\VBoxManage.exe" list hostonlyifs
```

This will show the IP address and network mask for the VirtualBox host-only network. Typical home and office ranges may look like:

- `192.168.0.0 - 192.168.255.255`

For the purpose of this lab, I will use a private network such as `192.168.x.x/24` as an example.

## Step 4: Download Windows Server 2022 ISO

Download the Windows Server 2022 ISO from Microsoft:

https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/evalcenter/download-windows-server-2022

This is the Windows operating system version that I will use for the environment.

Note: This is a 180-day free trial evaluation edition. After 180 days, you can reapply and renew the trial if needed.

## Step 5: Create the Domain Controller (DC01)

Open VirtualBox.

Click "New" and configure the VM as follows:
- VM Name: `DC01`
- VM Folder: use the default folder
- ISO Image: select the Windows Server 2022 ISO downloaded in Step 4
- Leave all other fields as default

Uncheck "Proceed with Unattended Installation" because we want to install it manually and add a few things ourselves. Then click "Next".

Set the following values:
- Base Memory / RAM: 4096 MB
- Number of CPUs: 2
- Disk Size: 60 GB

Then click "Next".

Review the VM summary and verify the values are correct.

### Network Adapter Configuration

Go to the VM settings and make sure you are in "Expert" mode. You can do this by clicking the menu option at the top of the settings page.

Set Adapter 1 to:
- Attached to: NAT

Click on Adapter 2 and check the box for "Enable Network Adapter".

Then set:
- Attach to: Host-Only Ethernet Adapter

This gives the domain controller a private network for communication with the rest of the lab while still allowing outbound access via NAT.

